course: biology
lesson: 78
title: Conservation: how people help animals come back
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Conservation means protecting living things and their homes so that animals on the edge can come back, and it works: bald eagles, humpback whales, red kites and Arabian oryx have all returned from the brink.
explain: The recipe is usually simple to say and hard to do: stop the killing, protect and join up the homes, remove new dangers like rats or poison, breed animals safely and set them free again.
explain: Cut a small hole at the bottom of a garden fence and you have just built part of a hedgehog highway; picking up fishing line on a beach clean is real conservation too.
explain: In cartoons one hero saves a species in an afternoon; in real life it takes scientists, rangers, zoo keepers and ordinary people working for decades, with every kakapo named and every condor counted.
fact: Bald eagles fell to about 400 to 500 nesting pairs in the lower 48 US states by the 1960s, partly because the pesticide DDT, building up from tiny water bugs into the fish and then into the eagles that ate them, made their eggshells too thin; after DDT was banned in 1972 they recovered to well over 300,000 birds.
fact: Commercial whaling killed huge numbers of whales until a worldwide pause began in 1986, and humpbacks have since recovered strongly in several oceans, though slowly, because whales live long and have few calves; male humpbacks sing long songs that spread across oceans.
fact: Protecting and joining up bamboo forests in China helped giant pandas, which moved from endangered to vulnerable in 2016 when wild numbers grew to about 1,800.
fact: Wolves were wiped out from Yellowstone by the 1920s, and 31 were brought back from Canada in 1995 and 1996; elk changed where they grazed, letting some riverside trees grow again, though scientists argue how big the effect was.
fact: A wild beaver family on the River Otter in Devon was allowed to stay in 2020; beaver dams slow rivers, hold water in droughts, reduce flooding downstream and make ponds full of wildlife, so beavers are called ecosystem engineers: they change the land around them more than almost any animal but us.
fact: Red kites were almost wiped out in England and Scotland by poisoning and shooting; so few were left that birds of the same kind from Spain and Sweden were released from 1989, and there are now thousands of pairs.
fact: The kakapo is a flightless, nocturnal New Zealand parrot, the heaviest in the world; it nests on the ground, where rats and stoats eat its eggs and chicks, and only 51 were known in 1995, but on predator-free islands with every bird named and checked, numbers have risen to around 250.
fact: By 1987 only 27 California condors were left, all caught for breeding because lead from bullets in carcasses poisoned them; there are now more than 500, over half flying free.
fact: Good zoos keep family records called studbooks so that rare animals are not too closely related, because a healthy mix of genes helps them survive disease and change.
fact: The Arabian oryx was hunted to extinction in the wild by 1972, but zoo-bred animals were released in Oman from 1982, and it moved from endangered to vulnerable in 2011; seen from the side its two long straight horns can look like one, and some think the unicorn legend came from it.
fact: Hedgehogs walk about 1 to 2 kilometres a night, and a hole about 13 centimetres square in a fence lets them pass between gardens along a hedgehog highway.
fact: Beach cleans stop plastic washing back into the sea, and counting the rubbish helps scientists find where it comes from; lost fishing nets, called ghost gear, can keep catching animals for years.
exercise: What made bald eagle eggshells so thin they cracked? | The pesticide DDT building up in the fish they ate | Very cold winters in the northern states that froze the eggs | Eagles eating too many eggshells | Noise from aeroplanes overhead
exercise: When was DDT banned in the USA? | 1972 | 1872 | 2007 | 1995
exercise: What helped humpback whales recover? | A worldwide pause on commercial whaling from 1986 | Feeding them by hand in big special pools along the coast | Moving them all to one ocean | Teaching them new songs
exercise: What helped giant panda numbers rise? | Protecting and joining up their bamboo forests | Feeding them pizza and cake in the big zoos of the world | Moving them all to Africa | Teaching them to eat meat
exercise: In 2016 the giant panda moved from endangered to which category? | Vulnerable | Extinct in the wild | Least concern | Critically endangered
exercise: How many wolves were brought back to Yellowstone in 1995 and 1996? | 31 | 3 | 310 | 3,100
exercise: Which of these is a way beaver dams help the land? | They hold water in droughts and reduce flooding downstream | They make rivers flow faster and straighter all the way to the sea | They keep all fish out of streams | They dry out marshes for farms
exercise: Why are beavers called ecosystem engineers? | They change the land around them more than almost any animal but us | They build real machines out of sticks and mud to cut the trees down | They work for the river authorities | They fix broken bridges
exercise: Where did the red kites released in England from 1989 come from? | Spain and Sweden | Canada and the USA | Australia and New Zealand | Mexico and Argentina
exercise: What is a kakapo? | A heavy, flightless, nocturnal parrot from New Zealand | A small flying owl from Australia that hunts mice at night in the bush | A kind of kiwi with a long beak | A green lizard from Madagascar
exercise: How did people help the kakapo? | Kept them on predator-free islands and checked every bird | Taught them to fly away from rats and cats by training them every day | Moved them all into city parks | Fed them to make them faster
exercise: What poisoned California condors? | Lead from bullets in animal carcasses | Salt from the sea air blowing in over the cliffs | Eating too much rotten fruit in the desert | Breathing in pollen from desert flowers
exercise: What is a studbook in a zoo? | A family record so rare animals are not too closely related | A book of rules telling zoo visitors what they may and may not do | A list of the animals' favourite foods | A guide to zoo opening times
exercise: Where was the Arabian oryx first released back into the wild? | Oman, from 1982 | Kenya, from 1950 | India, from 2011 | Mexico, from 1972
exercise: How big a hole in a fence lets hedgehogs through? | About 13 centimetres square | About 1 centimetre square | About 1 metre square | About 50 centimetres square
exercise: What is ghost gear? | Lost fishing line and nets that keep catching animals | Haunted ships that are said to sink every night at sea | Old diving suits left on beaches | Invisible fishing boats
exercise: Why do beach cleaners write down what they find? | Counting the rubbish helps scientists find where it comes from | To sell the rubbish later | To win a prize for whoever picks up the most rubbish on the beach | It is not useful, just a tradition
test: In a cartoon a superhero saves the pandas in one afternoon. What is real conservation like? | Many people working for years to protect and join up their homes | One person moving all of the pandas to a new island far away from people | Pandas saving themselves by learning to eat meat | A single law that makes pandas multiply
test: «poison in tiny water bugs can't hurt a massive eagle» What is right? | It builds up in the fish that eat the bugs, then in the eagles | It vanishes before it reaches anything as big as an eagle or a bear | Only tiny animals can be poisoned | Eagles only eat clean food from the sky
test: «why'd they bring wolves back, wolves just kill stuff» How can a predator help plants? | Elk changed where they grazed, so some riverside trees grew again | Wolves dig holes with their paws and bury seeds, which grow into trees | Wolves eat weeds that choke the trees | Wolves scare away the rain
test: «a condor never touched a bullet, so how'd it get poisoned» What is right? | It ate carcasses with lead bullets inside | It flew through the gun smoke | It drank from a river near a shooting range | Condors cannot be poisoned at all
test: What would happen if a zoo bred only brothers and sisters for many generations? | The animals would be less healthy and less able to survive disease | They would grow stronger and healthier with every new generation born | Nothing would change at all | They would turn into a new species
test: «why'd they use kites from Spain, not English ones» What is the best reason? | Almost none were left in England, and Spanish ones are the same species | Spanish kites are a bigger and better species that hunts more rabbits | English kites cannot fly over motorways | Spain wanted to get rid of its kites
test: «putting a hole in my fence, crap, the dog'll get out» What is right? | A hole about 13 cm square lets hedgehogs through but not most pets | Hedgehogs need a hole the size of a door | Hedgehogs climb fences easily, so you don't need a hole at all, mate | Dogs never fit through any hole
test: Why do rangers remove rats and stoats from kakapo islands? | Kakapos nest on the ground, so these predators eat eggs and chicks | Rats steal kakapo food from trees | Stoats sing too loudly at night and keep the kakapos awake until dawn | Rats and stoats are endangered and need moving
test: «the oryx is a unicorn, mate» What is right? | Some think the unicorn legend came from an oryx seen side-on | Yes, an oryx has just the one horn in the middle of its head | Yes, oryx are magic and glow in the dark | No, the oryx has no horns at all
test: «beaver dams flood stuff, they're pests» What else do the dams do? | Slow rivers, hold water in droughts and reduce flooding further down | Nothing useful at all | Make rivers dry up completely | Poison the frogs and insects
test: Why do whales take a long time to recover their numbers? | They live long and have few calves | Whales only breed once in their life | Whales need to learn new songs first | Whales are mostly male, with few females
exam: Identify the principal cause of the decline of the bald eagle in the United States during the mid-twentieth century. | The accumulation of DDT, which caused the thinning of eggshells | A succession of exceptionally severe winters | Competition for food with the California condor over the western mountains | The introduction of rats to their nesting sites
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the reintroduction of wolves to Yellowstone National Park. | Thirty-one wolves were translocated from Canada in 1995 and 1996 | Three hundred wolves were bred in zoos and released in 1872 | Wolves had never been absent from the park | Wolves were introduced from Spain and Sweden in 1989
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of a zoo studbook? | It records lineage so that rare animals are not paired with close relatives | It records the dietary preferences of each animal | It sets out regulations for the conduct of visitors | It records the opening hours of each enclosure
exam: Ascertain the number of California condors known to remain in the year 1987. | Twenty-seven | Two hundred and seventy | Fifty-one thousand | Five hundred thousand
